WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.253
Organizations use OSINT because significant security and risk information often appears outside their internal systems.

00:00:07.253 --> 00:00:17.143
Public sources may reveal exposed services, impersonation attempts, changes in business relationships, emerging threats, infrastructure dependencies, or events that affect operations.

00:00:17.143 --> 00:00:24.835
Security and threat-intelligence teams use public reporting to understand adversary behavior, vulnerabilities, campaigns, and defensive priorities.

00:00:24.835 --> 00:00:30.769
Incident responders may compare internal observations with public indicators or vendor advisories.

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:37.143
Fraud teams and due-diligence professionals may evaluate claims, ownership, reputation, and business risk.

00:00:37.143 --> 00:00:46.154
Journalists, researchers, and risk managers use public records, reporting, imagery, and time-based evidence to verify events and explain relationships.

00:00:46.154 --> 00:00:56.923
The same source can serve different purposes, so analysts must state the question and avoid treating collection as proof of a predetermined conclusion.

00:00:56.923 --> 00:01:02.857
Public information can expose patterns that are invisible in a single record.

00:01:02.857 --> 00:01:13.187
A domain registration change, a company announcement, a code-repository update, and an official filing may become meaningful when placed on a timeline.

00:01:13.187 --> 00:01:19.121
Relationships can also be misunderstood when names, dates, or identifiers are ambiguous.

00:01:19.121 --> 00:01:21.099
Context determines significance.

00:01:21.099 --> 00:01:27.472
An internet address may be shared, reassigned, or part of a hosted service.

00:01:27.472 --> 00:01:32.527
An old profile may no longer describe a person's role.

00:01:32.527 --> 00:01:38.901
An image may be genuine but unrelated to the claimed time or location.

00:01:38.901 --> 00:01:42.637
Findings should account for these alternative explanations.

00:01:42.637 --> 00:01:49.890
Good OSINT reduces uncertainty enough to support a decision; it does not promise perfect knowledge.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:59.780
Analysts should explain what is known, what remains uncertain, why the finding matters, and what additional information would change the assessment.
